<article xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="brief-report" dtd-version="1.3"><?properties open_access?><processing-meta base-tagset="archiving" mathml-version="3.0" table-model="xhtml" tagset-family="jats"><restricted-by>pmc</restricted-by></processing-meta><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Arthrosc Tech</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="iso-abbrev">Arthrosc Tech</journal-id><journal-title-group><journal-title>Arthroscopy Techniques</journal-title></journal-title-group><issn pub-type="epub">2212-6287</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Elsevier</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta>
<article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">41220680</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">12598225</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pii">S2212-6287(25)00394-9</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="doi">10.1016/j.eats.2025.103825</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="publisher-id">103825</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Technical Note</subject><subj-group><subject>Shoulder</subject></subj-group></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Subscapularis Repair Technique With Biphasic Cancellous Allograft in Anatomic Total Shoulder Arthroplasty</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au1"><name><surname>Puckett</surname><given-names>Caleb D.</given-names></name><degrees>B.S.</degrees><email>cdpucket@wakehealth.edu</email><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="cor1" ref-type="corresp">&#x02217;</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au2"><name><surname>Brady</surname><given-names>Davis</given-names></name><degrees>B.S.</degrees><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au3"><name><surname>Thiel</surname><given-names>Kaelli</given-names></name><degrees>M.D.</degrees><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au4"><name><surname>Perry</surname><given-names>Nichole M.</given-names></name><degrees>M.D.</degrees><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au5"><name><surname>Rogalski</surname><given-names>Brandon L.</given-names></name><degrees>M.D.</degrees><xref rid="aff3" ref-type="aff">c</xref></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author" id="au6"><name><surname>Reynolds</surname><given-names>Alan W.</given-names></name><degrees>M.D.</degrees><xref rid="aff1" ref-type="aff">a</xref><xref rid="aff2" ref-type="aff">b</xref></contrib><aff id="aff1"><label>a</label>Wake Forest University School of Medicine, Winston-Salem, North Carolina, U.S.A.</aff><aff id="aff2"><label>b</label>Department of Orthopaedic Surgery and Rehabilitation Atrium Health Wake Forest Baptist, Winston-Salem, North Carolina, U.S.A.</aff><aff id="aff3"><label>c</label>Department of Orthopaedic Surgery, Medical University of South Carolina Health, Charleston, South Carolina, U.S.A.</aff></contrib-group><author-notes><corresp id="cor1"><label>&#x02217;</label>Address correspondence to Caleb D. Puckett, B.S., 150 Peters Creek Parkway, Apt. 621, Winston-Salem, NC 27101, U.S.A. <email>cdpucket@wakehealth.edu</email></corresp></author-notes><pub-date pub-type="pmc-release"><day>22</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><!-- PMC Release delay is 0 months and 0 days and was based on <pub-date
						pub-type="epub">.--><pub-date pub-type="collection"><month>10</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><pub-date pub-type="epub"><day>22</day><month>8</month><year>2025</year></pub-date><volume>14</volume><issue>10</issue><elocation-id>103825</elocation-id><history><date date-type="received"><day>19</day><month>5</month><year>2025</year></date><date date-type="accepted"><day>16</day><month>7</month><year>2025</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>&#x000a9; 2025 The Authors</copyright-statement><copyright-year>2025</copyright-year><license><ali:license_ref xmlns:ali="http://www.niso.org/schemas/ali/1.0/" specific-use="textmining" content-type="ccbyncndlicense">https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/</ali:license_ref><license-p>This is an open access article under the CC BY-NC-ND license (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/).</license-p></license></permissions><abstract id="abs0010"><p>Subscapularis failure and insufficiency remain a devastating complication of anatomic total shoulder arthroplasty. In this Technical Note, we present a subscapularis repair with double-row compression and a biphasic cancellous allograft using a single anchor. Four holes are drilled in quadrilateral orientation at the lesser tuberosity. Two suture tapes are used to perform running, locking stitches in the subscapularis tendon, which are subsequently shuttled through the lesser tuberosity bone tunnels once, followed by a second passage back through the tendon and exchanging sides, to create a transosseous box and cross-compression of the tendon. Initial suture passage before tensioning creates an enclosure for placement of a biphasic cancellous allograft. Final tensioning and fixation into a single lateral anchor provides compression of the repair to the tuberosity, as well as secure fixation of the allograft at the tendon-bone interface. The use of a biphasic cancellous allograft has shown in vivo efficacy in reproducing the fibrocartilaginous interface seen in a native enthesis. This Technical Note discusses a viable technique for subscapularis repair in the total shoulder arthroplasty procedure with use of a biphasic cancellous allograft in effort to restore properties of the native enthesis to optimize subscapularis healing and integrity.</p></abstract><abstract abstract-type="graphical" id="abs0015"><title>Technique Video</title><p><supplementary-material content-type="local-data" id="mmc1a"><media xlink:href="mmc1.mp4"/></supplementary-material></p></abstract></article-meta></front><body><p id="p0010">Anatomic total shoulder arthroplasty (TSA), first performed by E. J. P&#x000e8;an in 1893, is indicated for the treatment of primary or secondary glenohumeral osteoarthritis in the setting of intact rotator cuff tendons or reparable rotator cuff tendinopathy.<xref rid="bib1" ref-type="bibr"><sup>1</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib2" ref-type="bibr"><sup>2</sup></xref> Conventionally, the TSA procedure requires releasing the subscapularis tendon through tenotomy, subscapularis peel, or lesser tuberosity osteotomy, with subsequent repair after implantation of the prosthesis.<xref rid="bib3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>, <xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>, <xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref>, <xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr">6</xref> However, less-common subscapularis-sparing techniques have been described that do not involve detachment of the subscapularis tendon from the lesser tuberosity.<xref rid="bib3" ref-type="bibr">3</xref>, <xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr">4</xref>, <xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr">5</xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib7" ref-type="bibr"><sup>7</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib8" ref-type="bibr"><sup>8</sup></xref> Poor outcomes including pain, impaired mobility, instability, and early implant failure may result from inadequate repair of the subscapularis tendon after the TSA procedure.<xref rid="bib4" ref-type="bibr"><sup>4</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib5" ref-type="bibr"><sup>5</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib9" ref-type="bibr"><sup>9</sup></xref> Failure rates as high as 6.7% to 47.2% of subscapular repair after TSA have been reported previously in the literature.<xref rid="bib9" ref-type="bibr">9</xref>, <xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr">10</xref>, <xref rid="bib11" ref-type="bibr">11</xref>, <xref rid="bib12" ref-type="bibr">12</xref>, <xref rid="bib13" ref-type="bibr">13</xref>, <xref rid="bib14" ref-type="bibr">14</xref>, <xref rid="bib15" ref-type="bibr">15</xref> After the TSA procedure, subscapularis deficiency can occur as the result of oversized implants, inadequate repair techniques, insufficient physical therapy, trauma, degenerative failure of the subscapular repair, or poor-quality tissue.<xref rid="bib10" ref-type="bibr"><sup>10</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib16" ref-type="bibr"><sup>16</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib17" ref-type="bibr"><sup>17</sup></xref> Furthermore, an adequate subscapularis repair is essential to the success of the TSA procedure. Because of the importance of this aspect of the procedure, various repair techniques have been described in the literature, including those with bioinductive patch augmentation.<xref rid="bib6" ref-type="bibr"><sup>6</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib18" ref-type="bibr">18</xref>, <xref rid="bib19" ref-type="bibr">19</xref>, <xref rid="bib20" ref-type="bibr">20</xref>, <xref rid="bib21" ref-type="bibr">21</xref> At the site of a native enthesis, a fibrocartilage transitional region is present, containing a transitional sequence of cell types, matrix composition, and tissue organization, which allows for the mechanical properties seen at the tendon-to-bone interface. However, after traditional tendon repair, fibrovascular scar tissue is formed at the insertion site, which has differing mechanical properties to that of the native enthesis.<xref rid="bib22" ref-type="bibr"><sup>22</sup></xref> The purpose of this Technical Note is to describe our technique for a subscapularis repair in a TSA procedure using a biphasic cancellous allograft, which has yet to be described. The Biphasic Cancellous Allograft (Bioenthesis, Madison, NJ) used is designed to provide a porous matrix that allows for migration of bone marrow from the humerus to the tendon, which lacks vascular supply.<xref rid="bib23" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref> This is designed to reproduce the transition from bone to soft tissue seen in the native enthesis.<xref rid="bib23" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref> The repair technique is designed to be implemented in the setting of a stemless humeral component.</p><sec id="sec1"><title>Surgical Technique</title><p id="p0015">The patient is placed in the beach-chair position with all bony prominences well padded (<xref rid="appsec1" ref-type="sec">Video 1</xref>). A standard deltopectoral skin approach is made, including a suprapectoral, soft-tissue biceps tenodesis. The subscapularis tendon is managed using a peel, confluently with the anterior capsule, and the upper border of the tendon is marked with a suture. Standard subscapularis releases of the rotator interval, middle glenohumeral ligament, and inferior capsule are performed to improve glenoid exposure and subscapularis excursion, for later reduction and repair to the lesser tuberosity.</p><p id="p0020">Anatomic total shoulder arthroplasty is performed using a cemented, hybrid inlay glenoid, and a stemless humeral component (INHANCE Shoulder System; DePuy Synthes, Raynham, MA). After trialing is completed, the subscapularis repair is prepared before final implantation of the humeral component.</p><p id="p0025">The surgical implants and materials needed for this subscapularis repair technique include 2 suture tapes (Arthrex, Naples, FL), 4 FiberLink SutureTapes (Arthrex), a 2.0 drill bit, a free needle, an Anchor Suture SwiveLock C Knotless 3.9 &#x000d7; 17.9 mm Cannula Full Thread Twist-In Nonabsorbable Vent Rotator (Arthrex), and a Biphasic Cancellous Allograft.<xref rid="bib23" ref-type="bibr"><sup>23</sup></xref></p><p id="p0030">To begin the repair preparation, 4 holes are drilled in a quadrilateral orientation 15 mm apart within the lesser tuberosity. The holes are placed at the superior lateral, superior medial, inferior lateral, and inferior medial aspects of the subscapularis tendon footprint (<xref rid="fig1" ref-type="fig">Fig 1</xref>). A FiberLink suture is passed into the superior medial drill hole and out the superior lateral hole. A second FiberLink suture is then passed through the inferior medial drill hole and out of the inferior lateral hole (<xref rid="fig2" ref-type="fig">Fig 2</xref>). At this point, the final humeral head component is implanted and the glenohumeral joint is reduced.<fig id="fig1"><label>Fig 1</label><caption><p>Four holes are drilled in a quadrilateral orientation 15 mm apart within the lesser tuberosity. The holes are placed at the superior lateral (gray arrow), superior medial (black arrow), inferior lateral (blue arrow), and inferior medial (white arrow) aspects of the subscapularis tendon footprint.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Fig 2</label><caption><p>A link suture is passed into the superior medial drill hole and out the superior lateral hole (gray arrow). A second link suture is then passed through the inferior medial drill hole and out of the inferior lateral hole (white arrow).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr2"/></fig></p><p id="p0035">Two running, locking stitches are then placed in the subscapularis tendon using 1.3-mm suture tape (Arthrex), resulting in 4 strands exiting the deep surface of the subscapularis tendon, approximately 15 mm from the most lateral edge (<xref rid="fig3" ref-type="fig">Fig 3</xref>). At the same level on the subscapularis tendon, one FiberLink suture is placed at both the superior and inferior portion of the tendon for future shuttling (<xref rid="fig4" ref-type="fig">Fig 4</xref>). The 2 superior suture tape tails and the superior link suture are then passed through the previously drilled and passed superior bone tunnel, while the 2 inferior suture tape tails and the inferior link suture are passed through the inferior bone tunnel using the respective link sutures previously passed through the bone tunnels (<xref rid="fig5" ref-type="fig">Fig 5</xref>). Once through the bone tunnels (<xref rid="fig6" ref-type="fig">Fig 6</xref>), one superior and one inferior suture tape tail are passed over top and back through the tendon using the superior link suture, and the other 2 suture tape tails similarly are passed back through the inferior tendon and bone tunnel (<xref rid="fig7" ref-type="fig">Fig 7</xref>). This creates a transosseous box and cross configuration for compression of the tendon over the lesser tuberosity (<xref rid="fig8" ref-type="fig">Fig 8</xref>). Before tensioning and fixation, this also creates an enclosure for allograft placement (<xref rid="fig9" ref-type="fig">Fig 9</xref>). At this point, a Biphasic Cancellous Allograft is placed within this enclosure, between the deep surface of the tendon and the tuberosity (<xref rid="fig10" ref-type="fig">Fig 10</xref>). The suture tape limbs are then placed into a 3.9-mm SwiveLock anchor and tensioned to complete reduction and compression of the subscapularis tendon to the lesser tuberosity, as well as secure fixation of the allograft between the 2 surfaces (<xref rid="fig11" ref-type="fig">Fig 11</xref>). Radiographs are taken to confirm placement of TSA components, as seen in <xref rid="fig12" ref-type="fig">Fig 12</xref>, <xref rid="fig13" ref-type="fig">Fig 13</xref> and <xref rid="fig12" ref-type="fig">Fig 12</xref>, <xref rid="fig13" ref-type="fig">Fig 13</xref>.<fig id="fig3"><label>Fig 3</label><caption><p>Two running, locking stitches are then placed in the subscapularis tendon using 1.3-mm suture tape at the superior (black lines) and inferior (white lines) portions of the tendon. This results in 4 strands exiting the deep surface of the subscapularis tendon, approximately 15 mm from the most lateral edge.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr3"/></fig><fig id="fig4"><label>Fig 4</label><caption><p>At the same level on the subscapularis tendon, one link suture is placed at both the superior (gray loop) and inferior (blue loop) portion of the tendon for future shuttling.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr4"/></fig><fig id="fig5"><label>Fig 5</label><caption><p>Using the respective link sutures (green loops) previously passed through the bone tunnels, the 2 superior suture tape tails (black lines) and the superior link suture (gray line) are then passed through the superior fiber loop (green loop), whereas the 2 inferior suture tape tails (white lines) and the inferior link suture (blue line) are passed through the inferior fiber loop (green loop).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr5"/></fig><fig id="fig6"><label>Fig 6</label><caption><p>The 2 superior suture tape tails (black lines) and the superior link suture (gray line) are now passed through the previously passed superior bone tunnel, and 2 inferior suture tape tails (white lines) and inferior link suture (blue line) are now passed through the inferior bone tunnel.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr6"/></fig><fig id="fig7"><label>Fig 7</label><caption><p>Once through the bone tunnels, one superior (black line) and one inferior (white line) suture tape tail are passed over top and back through the tendon using the superior link suture (gray loop), and the other 2 suture tape tails similarly back through the inferior link suture (blue loop).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr7"/></fig><fig id="fig8"><label>Fig 8</label><caption><p>A transosseous box and cross configuration for compression of the tendon over the lesser tuberosity is formed.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr8"/></fig><fig id="fig9"><label>Fig 9</label><caption><p>Before tensioning and fixation, an enclosure (white arrow) for the allograft (black arrow) placement can be seen.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr9"/></fig><fig id="fig10"><label>Fig 10</label><caption><p>At this point, a Biphasic Cancellous Allograft (white star) is placed within this enclosure, between the deep surface of the tendon and the tuberosity.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr10"/></fig><fig id="fig11"><label>Fig 11</label><caption><p>The suture tape limbs are then placed into a 3.9-mm SwiveLock anchor and tensioned to complete reduction and compression of the subscapularis tendon to the lesser tuberosity, as well as secure fixation of the allograft between the 2 surfaces.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr11"/></fig><fig id="fig12"><label>Fig 12</label><caption><p>Anteroposterior Grashey view: radiograph imaging 6 months postoperatively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr12"/></fig><fig id="fig13"><label>Fig 13</label><caption><p>Axillary view: radiograph imaging 6 months postoperatively.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="gr13"/></fig></p></sec><sec id="sec2"><title>Discussion</title><p id="p0040">Subscapularis mobilization during the TSA procedure via tenotomy, subscapularis peel, and lesser tuberosity osteotomy have all been described previously in the literature. Although a systematic review of biomechanical cadaveric studies reported superior biomechanical outcomes after lesser tuberosity osteotomy, another systematic review reported no distinct differences in subscapularis strength, range of motion, or functional outcomes scores between the subscapularis release techniques in the TSA procedure.<xref rid="bib24" ref-type="bibr"><sup>24</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib25" ref-type="bibr"><sup>25</sup></xref> Meyers et al.<xref rid="bib18" ref-type="bibr"><sup>18</sup></xref> has previously described a similar subscapularis fixation technique, reporting no clinical failures for almost 2 years of its use, at the time of publication.</p><p id="p0045">Bailey et al.<xref rid="bib26" ref-type="bibr"><sup>26</sup></xref> conducted a meta-analysis in which they concluded that rotator cuff repair (RCR) with graft augmentation or interposition, when compared with RCR alone, appears to have resulted in improved American Shoulder and Elbow Surgeons scores and lower retear rates. However, there is a paucity of clinical studies that investigate RCR with use of the biphasic cancellous allograft described in this technique. Bone-to-bone healing, when compared with tendon-to-bone healing, is shown to be superior in mitigating structural failure.<xref rid="bib27" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref><sup>,</sup><xref rid="bib28" ref-type="bibr"><sup>28</sup></xref> Tendon-to-bone healing may be inferior because of the lack of native enthesis regeneration after repair, which has been shown in multiple studies in animals.<xref rid="bib28" ref-type="bibr">28</xref>, <xref rid="bib29" ref-type="bibr">29</xref>, <xref rid="bib30" ref-type="bibr">30</xref>, <xref rid="bib31" ref-type="bibr">31</xref> Dickerson et al.<xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref> investigated the in vitro mechanical properties and the in vivo efficacy of a tissue scaffold constructed from bovine cancellous bone in regeneration of the bone-to-soft tissue interface. They concluded that the study provided evidence that the cancellous scaffold derived from bone promotes the growth of collagenous tissue, reproducing the fibrocartilaginous interface seen in a native enthesis, while also reducing the formation of enthesophytes.<xref rid="bib32" ref-type="bibr"><sup>32</sup></xref> The use of a biphasic cancellous allograft for supraspinatus repair has been previously described; however, its use in subscapularis tendon repair has yet to be described in the literature.<xref rid="bib27" ref-type="bibr"><sup>27</sup></xref> Pearls and pitfalls of the technique as well as advantages and disadvantages are presented in <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>, <xref rid="tbl2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref> and <xref rid="tbl1" ref-type="table">Table 1</xref>, <xref rid="tbl2" ref-type="table">Table 2</xref>, respectively.<table-wrap position="float" id="tbl1"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Pearls and Pitfalls</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th>Pearls</th><th>Pitfalls</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ensure looped end of passing sutures remains on the bursal side</td><td>Anchor trajectory may become blocked by the implant</td></tr><tr><td>Only partially tension repair before placing the interposition graft</td><td>Passing sutures placed too lateral within the subscapularis tendon</td></tr><tr><td>Drill lesser tuberosity tunnels before implant placement</td><td/></tr><tr><td>Measure and cut the graft after drilling bone tunnels</td><td/></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap position="float" id="tbl2"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Advantages and Disadvantages</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th>Advantages</th><th>Disadvantages</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interposition biphasic graft to optimize tendon to bone healing</td><td>Cost of graft and anchor</td></tr><tr><td>Tensionable repair with single anchor</td><td>Requires planning and suture management</td></tr><tr><td>Adjustable enclosure in which to place the graft</td><td/></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap></p></sec><sec sec-type="COI-statement" id="coi0010"><title>Disclosures</title><p id="p0050">All authors (C.D.P., D.B., K.T., N.M.P., B.L.R., A.W.R.) declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper.</p></sec></body><back><ref-list id="cebib0010"><title>References</title><ref id="bib1"><label>1</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref1"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Lugli</surname><given-names>T.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Artificial shoulder joint by Pean (1893): T facts of an exceptional intervention and the prosthetic method</article-title><source>Clin Orthop Relat Res</source><volume>133</volume><year>1978</year><fpage>215</fpage><lpage>218</lpage></element-citation></ref><ref id="bib2"><label>2</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref2"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Mattei</surname><given-names>L.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mortera</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name><name><surname>Arrigoni</surname><given-names>C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Castoldi</surname><given-names>F.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Anatomic shoulder arthroplasty: An update on indications, technique, results and complication rates</article-title><source>Joints</source><volume>3</volume><year>2015</year><fpage>72</fpage><lpage>77</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">26605254</pub-id>
</element-citation></ref><ref id="bib3"><label>3</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref3"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ross</surname><given-names>J.P.</given-names></name><name><surname>Lee</surname><given-names>A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Neeley</surname><given-names>R.</given-names></name><name><surname>Mighell</surname><given-names>M.A.</given-names></name><name><surname>Frankle</surname><given-names>M.A.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The subscapularis-sparing windowed anterior technique for total shoulder arthroplasty</article-title><source>J Shoulder Elbow Surg</source><volume>30</volume><year>2021</year><fpage>S89</fpage><lpage>S99</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">33887479</pub-id>
</element-citation></ref><ref id="bib4"><label>4</label><element-citation publication-type="journal" id="sref4"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Piper</surname><given-names>C.C.</given-names></name><name><surname>Horneff</surname><given-names>J.G.</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>3rd. Management of subscapularis insufficiency after total shoulder arthroplasty</article-title><source>J Am Acad Orthop Surg</source><volume>30</volume><year>2022</year><fpage>933</fpage><lpage>940</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">36037282</pub-id>
